\section{Goal, and the answer up front}

The objective is to reproduce the measured He\,\textsc{i} $10830$\,\AA{}
transit line of LHS\,1140\,b with EXHALE, which solves the wind rather than
imposing it, and to establish what (if anything) has to be added to
the physics for the model to match the observation.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Everything below was re-solved on 2026-09-14.} The results
of 2026-08-30 that earlier revisions of this memo carried are superseded:
those states were never stationary below $1.1\,R_p$ under the residual the
code now applies, and the code itself has moved in ways that reach this wind
directly. What was done, why, and how far each number moved is
Sect.~\ref{sec:whatchanged}. Every EXHALE number below is read from the
model tree of record, \texttt{LHS1140b/models/}, catalogued in
\texttt{LHS1140b/MODELS.md}; a number that is still the archived one is
marked \emph{archived 2026-08-30} where it appears.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Result.} The \emph{amount} of absorption can be matched
over a prescribed metal-free scalar base, at a composition that depends on
what is assumed about the spectrum and about mixing. Over a lower atmosphere
solved as a photochemical column and handed over as a profile, C, N and O
included, it can no longer be matched from above: the lowest reservoir the
elemental-flux closure returns, $\mathrm{He/H} = 2.09$, already makes an
equivalent width $35$ per cent larger than the measured one, and the ladder
rises from there. The composition that base would need lies below the ladder
and has not been solved. The \emph{width} is not matched over either base,
and the velocity it asks for is the same number every time.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What moved the solved-column branch.} Two of the changes of
Sect.~\ref{sec:whatchanged} act on it together. The C, N and O line cooling
of the six ions the photochemical column brings into the base was evaluated
in the coronal limit, above the critical density of the levels it excites,
and is now the CHIANTI statistical equilibrium at the cell's own electron
density; the closed-form fits it replaces also mixed theoretical with
observed level energies, which at $2000$\,K is a factor $14$ in
$\exp(-\Delta E/kT)$ for O\,\textsc{ii}. \emph{The base metals are
therefore no longer a thermostat.} MEASURED at one composition,
$\mathrm{He/H} = 2.13$ over the scalar base with and without the column's
C, N and O reservoirs: $T_{\max}$ $5694 \to 5537$\,K and the red-pair
equivalent width $1.3934 \to 1.3917$\,\%\,\AA{}, a change of $0.12$ per
cent. The factor $2.6$ that the 2026-08-30 draft carried for this step is
gone.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{(i) The amount is always matchable, so what the line
returns is a conditional pair rather than a measurement.}

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{The prescribed-base branch.} With the base a scalar state
carrying no metals, every combination of assumed spectrum and assumed mixing
reaches the observed red-pair equivalent width at some helium abundance, and
the conditions stack inside this branch. In the well-mixed limit the
crossing is $\mathrm{He/H} = 0.4012$ ($0.3851$--$0.4201$ on the
measurement's $1\sigma$ interval) on the GJ\,1132 proxy spectrum and
$0.0526$ ($0.0510$--$0.0543$), below solar, on the GJ\,699 one: the second
stand-in for LHS\,1140's unobserved XUV, $5.1$ times brighter, which the
paper also uses. With binary H/He element diffusion active (helium then
settles out of the base unless an eddy term lifts it) it rises again, to
$2.72$ wherever $K_{zz} \le 10^{5}$\,cm$^2$\,s$^{-1}$ does nothing against
the molecular coefficient, $1.492$ at the adopted $K_{zz} = 10^{9}$ and
$0.878$ at $10^{11}$ (Sect.~\ref{sec:diffbroad}). The dependence on the
eddy coefficient is weak and monotonic, as $K_{zz}^{-0.114}$ over the four
decades where the eddy term acts at all (a least-squares fit to the four
crossings at $10^{8}$ to $10^{11}$); the dependence on the assumed spectrum
is a factor $7.6$.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{The solved-column branch, which no longer brackets the
measurement.} Replacing that base with a photochemical column (Photochem
with its climate step solved, handed to EXHALE as a profile at a
$1\,\mu$bar match, at the same $K_{zz} = 10^{9}$ and with the same
diffusion active) brings C, N and O into the base. Nine reservoirs were
closed on their own elemental fluxes, and the equivalent width they return
runs from $1.5006$\,\%\,\AA{} at $\mathrm{He/H} = 2.0924$ to
$2.2729$\,\%\,\AA{} at $12.0136$: every one of them stands above the
measured $1.108 \pm 0.030$, the lowest by $35$ per cent
(Sect.~\ref{sec:closureladder}). \emph{There is no crossing on this
ladder.} Read against the ladder's own local slope, $\mathrm{d}\ln
EW/\mathrm{d}\ln(\mathrm{He/H}) = 0.49$ between the two lowest rungs, the
measurement would be reached near $\mathrm{He/H} \approx 1.1$, which is
below every rung solved and is an extrapolation, not a solution. The
earlier crossing at $8.212$ was a consequence of the base metals cooling
the wind; they no longer do.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Neither branch is an independent prediction of the model.}
The prescribed-base number is the reservoir the observed line picks out over
its own base, and the escape rate (which is an output, not a fit) barely
moves along the way. The flux closure does not change that on the profile
branch: it returns $\mathrm{He/H}$ at the match from trial fluxes a factor
of ten apart, single-valued to a few parts in $10^{6}$
(Sect.~\ref{sec:closure}), but at $K_{zz} = 10^{9}$ the eddy term
homogenizes the column all the way to the microbar match, so the closure
hands back very nearly the reservoir it was given. What the closure adds is
that a composition is consistent with a photochemical lower atmosphere
carrying its own C, N and O, not that it is measured.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Why the same helium now absorbs almost equally over the two
bases.} MEASURED on the new tree at essentially one composition, the scalar
metal-free base at $\mathrm{He/H} = 2.13$ against the closed solution on the
photochemical column at $2.0924$: $T_{\max}$ $5694$ against $5382$\,K, the
metastable peak $34.7$ against $35.9$\,cm$^{-3}$, the metastable column over
$2$--$20\,R_p$ $45.7$ against $46.3$\,cm$^{-2}$ in units of $R_p$, and the
equivalent width $1.3934$ against $1.5006$\,\%\,\AA{}. The photochemical
column now makes the \emph{stronger} line, by $7.7$ per cent, where the
2026-08-30 solutions made it weaker by a factor $2.6$. Raising the reservoir
still reheats the wind, and far more strongly than before: at
$\mathrm{He/H} = 10.01$ the mean mass per particle at $2\,R_p$ is
$3.19\,m_{\rm H}$ against $2.16$, the same deposited energy is shared among
fewer and heavier particles, and $T_{\max}$ rises to $7673$\,K
(Sect.~\ref{sec:basemetals}, Fig.~\ref{fig:thermostat}).

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{The escape rate is flat on every axis, and the crossings span
a factor $52$.} $\log_{10}(\dot M/\mathrm{g\,s^{-1}}) = 7.84$--$7.88$ on the
GJ\,1132 proxy across composition, diffusion and eddy coefficient alike,
$7.91$--$7.92$ across the whole closed ladder, and $8.43$--$8.57$ on the
GJ\,699 proxy. Against that, the composition the line implies runs from
$0.0526$ to $2.72$ over choices this measurement cannot arbitrate: strongly
conditional on the assumed spectrum and on what is placed at the lower
boundary, weakly and monotonically on the assumed mixing. Any composition
read off this line (the paper's $\mathrm{H\!:\!He} \lesssim 10^{-3}$ as much
as any value here) inherits those conditions.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{(ii) The width is not reached on any axis, and the deficit
is invariant.} At the equivalent-width crossing the profile is $3.3$ times
too deep and $3.3$ times too narrow (red depth $4.105$ against the measured
$1.254$ per cent, FWHM $0.2533$ against $0.841$\,\AA), and no composition
fixes it, because composition changes how much helium absorbs, not how the
absorption is spread in velocity. Measured directly (convolving the solved
profile with a Gaussian and asking which one reproduces the observed line)
the missing spread is $22.22$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ FWHM
($\sigma = 9.44$\,km\,s$^{-1}$) in the well-mixed limit,
$22.22$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ with diffusion and the adopted $K_{zz}$,
$22.15$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ for the flux-closed solution on the solved
photochemical column, and $21.97$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ on the $5.1\times$ brighter
spectrum: a spread of $1.1$ per cent over four solutions that differ by a
factor $40$ in composition (Sects.~\ref{sec:broadening},
\ref{sec:diffbroad}, \ref{sec:closureladder} and~\ref{sec:gj699}).
Supplied as one Gaussian it puts depth, blue depth and red/blue ratio
inside the observed $1\sigma$ interval at once, with nothing tuned to them;
what produces it is unidentified. It is not thermal (the solved gas
cools below $1000$\,K over the line-forming region), and not bulk
outflow, which reaches $1.1$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ at $20\,R_p$ on the fainter
spectrum and $3.6$ on the brighter. Read as turbulence it is
$\mathcal{M} = 2.3$--$4.0$ against the sound speed these solutions
themselves carry, supersonic on every convention and well above what the
literature is willing to assume. The one mechanism that would supply width
without a free parameter (the adiabatic-cooling metastable bump of
Schulik \& Owen (2025)) is present in these solutions and measured, but
sits at $0.4$--$2.5$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ (Sect.~\ref{sec:bump}). The shortfall is
in the energy and momentum budget, not in the composition, and it is a
published problem rather than a defect of this setup
(Sect.~\ref{sec:width}).

The paper's own p-winds fit does not reproduce the line either. It is
broader than the wind solved here, because its temperature is imposed at
$5000$--$6000$\,K where the line forms, but at FWHM $0.575$\,\AA{} with its
turbulence term on ($0.458$ without it) it is still $1.5$ times narrower
than the measured $0.841$, and it overshoots the blue component threefold.
Adding broadening does not rescue it: its absorbing power is
$24\%$ below the measurement, so no convolution can reach the observed
depth and width at once. \emph{The width is a shortfall the two codes
share}, which is what the audit of the measurement side
(Sect.~\ref{sec:widthaudit}) leaves as the reading: the measurement is not
at fault, and neither one-dimensional steady-state wind reproduces it.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Two caveats that attach to the outer wind and not to the
line.} \emph{The domain stops inside the critical point.} These solutions
are the subsonic part of a transonic escape whose critical point sits at
$40.06\,R_p$, outside the $30\,R_p$ domain they are solved on, so the outer
ghost rule and not a critical point is what closes them. It costs the mass
flux almost nothing: $\log_{10}\dot M = 7.8737$, $7.8740$, $7.8735$ at
$30$, $45$ and $60\,R_p$, a spread of $5\times10^{-4}$\,dex over a factor
two in domain size, and the He\,\textsc{i} $10830$ equivalent width moves by
at most $1.7$ per cent. What it does cost is the outer profile: at
$28\,R_p$ the $30\,R_p$ solution is $25$ per cent too hot, $29$ per cent
too dense and $22$ per cent too slow against the converged answer, so
\emph{nothing above about $10\,R_p$ in these profiles should be quoted}
(Sect.~\ref{sec:validity}). \emph{The gas is not collisional out there
either}: $\mathrm{Kn}_{\rm bulk}$ passes $0.1$ at $7.3$--$7.5\,R_p$ and
reaches $0.67$--$0.70$ at $29\,R_p$, while the He\,$10830$ layer at
$1.1$--$3\,R_p$ sits at $\mathrm{Kn} \le 0.011$ and the line analysis is
untouched.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{A caveat that attaches to the composition.} Above about
$4\,R_p$ the ionization state of this wind is frozen into the flow: the
recombination and photoionization Damk\"ohler numbers fall to $10^{-2}$ and
below, so the photoionization equilibrium the wind is \emph{solved} with is
not the composition that gas carries. The advection-corrected \texttt{\_adv}
profiles the transit tool consumes say so, and the difference is not small:
the neutral hydrogen fraction differs by a factor $4.5$ at the outer
boundary, and the He\,\textsc{i} $10830$ equivalent width read from the
solution's own composition is $15.51$\,\%\,\AA{} against $1.17$ from the
corrected one, a factor $13.3$. \emph{Every line number in this memo is read
from the \texttt{\_adv} profiles}; the solution's own composition is not a
usable one above $4\,R_p$ and is not quoted. Carrying the three ionization
fractions as transported variables is designed but not implemented
(Sect.~\ref{sec:whatchanged}).

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Three systematics ride on the absolute numbers, and none of
them is caught by a convergence test.} They are stated here because they
bound how far any single crossing should be read.
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep0.2ex
\item \emph{The outer region is inherited from the seed and is not resolved
      by the domain.} A converged wind takes its $20$--$30\,R_p$ state from
      the solution it was started from, and $\|R\|$ cannot separate two such
      states. The domain study of Sect.~\ref{sec:validity} now bounds what
      that costs the line: moving the outer boundary to $45$ and $60\,R_p$
      moves the equivalent width by at most $1.7$ per cent and the mass flux
      by $5\times10^{-4}$\,dex, while the profiles above $10\,R_p$ move by
      $22$--$29$ per cent.
\item \emph{The closure tolerance is visible in the answer.} The closure
      stops at $\mathrm{tol} = 0.05$ on the undamped elemental residual, and
      the rungs of the ladder end at $0.045$ (helium) and $0.018$
      (hydrogen). Tightening it is refused by the driver, because the radial
      spread of the flux over the measurement window, $3.7\times10^{-3}$ in
      hydrogen and $4.9\times10^{-3}$ in helium, would then approach the
      residual being decided on it.
\item \emph{Which chemistry produced the column.} Every column here is
      Photochem 0.9.0 with the local corrections of
      \texttt{docs/photochem\_solver\_modification\_*.md}.
\end{enumerate}

