\section{What changed since 2026-08-30}
\label{sec:whatchanged}

Every EXHALE number in this memo was re-solved between 2026-09-13 and
2026-09-14. This section states why, what was changed in the code and in the
recipe, and how far the answers moved. The campaign itself, case by case,
is \texttt{LHS1140b/MODELS.md}; the plan it executed is
\texttt{docs/PLAN\_20260913\_lhs\_stationary.md} and its measurements are the
items \texttt{docs/lhs1140b\_stationary\_L*.md}.

\subsection{Why the 2026-08-30 solutions were discarded}

They were not stationary. Taken apart row by row on the current residual
(item L1), the archived state carries an $O(1)$ residual everywhere between
the base and about $1.1\,R_p$: in every cell below $1.049\,R_p$ the energy
row is dominated by its own flux divergence rather than by its radiative
terms ($|\mathrm{d}F_3|/|\Gamma - \Lambda| = 7\times10^{5}$ at cell 1,
$4.9\times10^{1}$ at cell 16), and the momentum row is out by a constant
$2.8$ per cent of the local weight over the whole column. That is a property
of the state, not of the diagnostic: the base-layer velocity sawtooth
contributes nothing to the energy row, and putting the archived heating back
into the same hydrodynamic state lowers the worst cell only from $1.979$ to
$1.930$. The old runs reported convergence because their flux gate began at
$1.2\,R_p$ and never looked below it.

Mapping such a state onto the current grid and re-solving does not repair
it. All four routes tried stagnate: the JFNK solve at
$\|R\| = 1.6$--$2.0$ for 56 iterations, the marching restart at a mass-flux
spread $du$ that never falls below the $10^{-2}$ hand-off in 5600 steps, and
the partitioned stationary route at \texttt{info} $= 1$ or $2$ with the
composition relaxation finding no admissible advance
(\texttt{MODELS.md} Sect.~6). Separately, the code has moved in ways that
reach this wind directly: the photon-grid quadrature (a $9.5$ per cent
overcount of the H\,\textsc{i} photoionization rate removed), the cell width
$\mathrm{d}r_j$, the Jupiter radius ($+2.26$ per cent in $R_0$, which also
changes the grid, so no archived state can even be reloaded without
interpolation), the helium mass, the CODATA constants, the spectrum below
$13.6$\,eV that the He($2\,^3S$) population depends on, and the ghost-row fix
of the transit reader.

\subsection{The well-balanced reconstruction, without which nothing certifies}

At the base of this planet the flow's own Mach number is
$2.7\times10^{-7}$. The contact speed the HLLC flux puts through a base
face is then set by the WENO3 reconstruction's third-order truncation error
in the face pressure rather than by the velocity --- one to three decades
above it --- so the energy row of every base cell measures its own largest
term against itself and no seed can lower it. A relative density jump $d$ at
a face enters the interface flux through its acoustic part as $d\,\rho c_s$,
which is $d/\mathcal{M}$ times $\rho v$; at $\mathcal{M} \sim 10^{-6}$ a
per-cent jump is a six-decade error in the flux.

\texttt{Well balanced:\ True} is the reconstruction of K\"appeli \& Mishra
(2016): every cell carries its own hydrostatic equilibrium, the
reconstruction and the Riemann solver act on the \emph{departure} from it,
and the momentum source is the face-pressure difference of that same
equilibrium, so at a discrete hydrostatic equilibrium the flux difference
and the source cancel in the algebra instead of in floating point. The
term-by-term statement, the mapping onto the code and the reason the key is
off by default are in \texttt{docs/well\_balanced\_and\_hydrostatic\_20260913}.
What it does here, same seed, same input, same binary (item L5c):

