\textbf{The full ladder is three regimes, not one power law}
(Fig.~\ref{fig:hehkzz}). Above $10^{7}$ the degeneracy is shallow and very
nearly a power law,
$\mathrm{d}\log(\mathrm{He/H})/\mathrm{d}\log K_{zz} = -0.114$
(a least-squares fit over $10^{8}$--$10^{11}$; $-0.109$, $-0.112$, $-0.116$
and $-0.114$ decade by decade), so
$\mathrm{He/H} \propto K_{zz}^{-0.114}$. Below $10^{7}$ the curve rolls
over ($-0.025$ across $10^{6}$--$10^{7}$, $-0.012$ across
$10^{5}$--$10^{6}$) onto a plateau at $\mathrm{He/H} = 2.72$, the value the
line implies when the eddy term does nothing.

\emph{The shape has survived every revision; the level has not.} Decade by
decade the exponent was $-0.021$, $-0.071$, $-0.130$, $-0.150$, $-0.151$ and
$-0.133$ on the 2026-08-30 solutions, against $-0.012$, $-0.025$, $-0.109$,
$-0.112$, $-0.116$ and $-0.114$ here: the three regimes and their ordering
are the same, the shallow branch is $20$ per cent shallower and the roll-off
below $10^{7}$ is steeper. What moved is the level, and not by a uniform
factor: the plateau falls to $0.71$ of its archived value while the
$10^{11}$ end rises to $1.05$ (last column of Table~\ref{tab:kzzcross}).
That is the shallow branch flattening, not a rescaling.

That plateau is where it is for a reason that is measured rather than
assumed: the operator adds $K_{zz}$ to the molecular coefficient, and the
base coefficient of the $K_{zz} = 0$ run is
$D_{\rm eff} = 1.3395\times10^{6}$\,cm$^2$\,s$^{-1}$ (measured at the
$r = 1.00029\,R_p$ face in \texttt{output/element\_flux\_profile.txt},
dominant pair He\,\textsc{i}--H\,\textsc{i}), so $K_{zz} = 10^{6}$ raises it
by $75\%$, $10^{5}$ by $7\%$ and $10^{4}$ by $0.7\%$. The crossings of the
table say the same thing from the other side: $10^{5}$ moves the inferred
reservoir by $0.35$ per cent against the $8.3$ per cent the measurement
error alone allows, and $10^{6}$ is the first decade that moves it by more
than that error. At the other end the operator never reaches the well-mixed
limit either: even at $10^{11}$ the inferred reservoir, $0.878$, is
$2.2$ times the well-mixed $0.4012$.

Over the span the literature leaves open at the base pressure of these runs
the inferred reservoir ratio moves only from $1.14$ to $1.93$, a factor
$1.7$, against a factor $7$ in equivalent width across the same two decades
at fixed composition: the line responds to composition much faster than it
responds to $K_{zz}$, so a large change in the eddy coefficient is bought
back by a small change in the reservoir. \emph{The composition inferred from
this line under diffusion is therefore only weakly conditional on $K_{zz}$,
as $K_{zz}^{-0.114}$, and strongly conditional on the assumed spectrum}: the
SED moves the well-mixed crossing by a factor $7.6$, from $0.4012$ to
$0.0526$ (Sect.~\ref{sec:gj699}), which is more than twice the entire
$K_{zz}$ lever.

