Figure~\ref{fig:beststruct} shows what that EXHALE solution actually is. It
is not a fast, hot outflow: the temperature peaks at $4282$\,K near
$1.51\,R_p$ (comparable to the isothermal value p-winds imposes), but
then falls by adiabatic expansion to $855$\,K by $10\,R_p$ over the region
that forms the line, and the velocity reaches only $1.1$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ at
$20\,R_p$. Both elements stay mostly neutral through the line-forming
region and helium is the \emph{more} ionized of the two, not the less:
$x(\mathrm{H\,\textsc{ii}}) = 0.03$, $0.07$ and $0.16$ at $2$, $3$ and
$5\,R_p$ against $x(\mathrm{He\,\textsc{ii}}+\mathrm{He\,\textsc{iii}}) =
0.06$, $0.14$ and $0.29$, since helium carries the electrons that make the
