\noindent\textbf{The base metals are no longer a thermostat setting.} With
the C, N and O line cooling evaluated at the cell's own electron density
rather than in the coronal limit (Sect.~\ref{sec:cnocooling}), adding the
column's elemental reservoirs to the scalar base at fixed composition costs
the wind $157$\,K in $T_{\max}$ and the line $0.12$ per cent of its
equivalent width. The comparison the 2026-08-30 solutions made between
columns one and three --- $T_{\max}$ $5447 \rightarrow 3955$\,K and $EW$
$1.424 \rightarrow 0.545$\,\%\,\AA, a factor $2.6$ --- was a consequence of
the coronal coefficient, which stood up to four decades above the
statistical equilibrium of the same ion at these densities. What is left
between the scalar base and the photochemical column at the same reservoir
is $312$\,K in $T_{\max}$ and $+7.7$ per cent in the line, and it is no
longer dominated by the metals: columns two and three still differ by the
matching-level temperature, the base radius and the elemental profile the
column carries.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What the base metals now cost the wind.} With no metals,
radiative cooling is $3.9$--$6.8$ per cent of the heating over
$1.2$--$2\,R_p$ and the wind is limited by expansion rather than by
radiation. With the column's C, N and O beneath it, cooling is
$4.7$--$7.8$ per cent, of which the metal lines are $21$ per cent at
$1.2\,R_p$ and $3$ per cent at $2\,R_p$, and $T_{\max}$ falls from $5694$ to
$5382$\,K. Both the metastable peak and the metastable column rise slightly
($34.6 \rightarrow 35.9$\,cm$^{-3}$ and $69.2 \rightarrow
70.8$\,cm$^{-3}R_p$ over $1$--$10\,R_p$), and the line rises with them. The
qualitative statement the 2026-08-30 draft made here --- that the metals
take the outer column, because the cooler wind has the smaller scale height
and does not carry the metastable population out to where it adds absorbing
area --- is a real mechanism at a cooling rate that large, but it is not what
these solutions do at the cooling rate the density-resolved coefficient
gives.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Raising the reservoir reheats it, by two terms that are
both measured.} From $2.09$ to $10.01$ on the same base, $T_{\max}$ goes
$5382 \rightarrow 7673$\,K. Two things change together. First, there are
fewer particles to share the deposited energy: the mean mass per particle at
$2\,R_p$ rises from $2.16$ to $3.19\,m_{\rm H}$, a factor $1.48$, and the
heating rate at $2\,R_p$ divided by the particle density rises by $1.50$,
with $n_{\rm tot}$ on the code's \texttt{calc\_ntot} convention, one
particle per species and electrons excluded, as elsewhere in this section,
while $T(2\,R_p)$ rises by $1.57$. The three factors agreeing to within
$6$ per cent is a consistency check on that reading, not a proof of it: the
cooling term is not held fixed between the two runs. Second, the coolant is
diluted along with the hydrogen it is referred to: the profile states C, N
and O per hydrogen nucleus (schema column \texttt{X\_*[El/H nuclei]}, and in
the code $n_X = \mathtt{melem\_ab}(e)\,n_{\rm H}$), and the two profiles
carry ${\rm C/H}$ within $0.1$ per cent of each other, so when
$n({\rm H\,\textsc{i}})$ at $2\,R_p$ falls by $3.8$ the carbon density falls
with it by the same factor. The volumetric metal cooling nonetheless
\emph{rises}, $2.40 \rightarrow 4.71\times10^{-11}$\,erg\,cm$^{-3}$\,s$^{-1}$
at $1.5\,R_p$, because the cooling function is steeply temperature
dependent; what the dilution does is let the gas settle at the higher
temperature at which that happens.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What the metastable balance does with that.} The 2\,$^3S$
population in these solutions is fed almost entirely by recombination and
drained by Penning ionization on neutral hydrogen and by electron collisional
de-excitation. Reconstructing the residual EXHALE solves: source
$n_e n_{\rm He\,\textsc{ii}}\alpha_{2^3S}$ plus collisional excitation from
the singlet ground state, against radiative decay $A_{31}$, photoionization,
destruction on H\,\textsc{i}, electron
collisional de-excitation to $2\,^1S$ and $2\,^1P$, and electron collisional
ionization (\texttt{ion\_residual\_core.f90}, \texttt{tr\_triplet\_row})
apportions the budget. At the metastable peak of the advection-corrected
profiles, collisional excitation supplies a negligible part of the source and
radiative decay plus collisional ionization take $0.02$--$0.03$ per cent of
the sink. What is left is: at ${\rm He/H} = 2.09$, Penning $72\%$ and
electron de-excitation $28\%$ of the loss; at $10.01$, Penning $53\%$ and
electron de-excitation $47\%$. Between the two the source rate rises by
$2.83$ (more He\,\textsc{ii} and more electrons in the hotter gas) while the
metastable lifetime is essentially unchanged, $1.12$ against $1.00$\,s: the
neutral hydrogen the Penning term feeds on is thinned by only $1.20$ at the
peak, and the faster electron de-excitation of the hotter gas takes back
what that gains. The product, $2.53$, is the peak ratio
$35.9 \rightarrow 91.0$\,cm$^{-3}$. \emph{There is no charge-exchange term
on the metastable in this code} (the He/H charge exchange acts on
ground-state He and He$^+$), so it is not part of this balance.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Read as an interpretation}, the thermal structure of the
$10.01$ solution ($T_{\max} = 7673$\,K, column $113.7$ over $1$--$10\,R_p$)
now stands well above that of the metal-free $2.13$ solution ($5694$\,K,
$69.2$), and its equivalent width is correspondingly above the measured
value rather than back at it. The ladder therefore still looks like a
thermal trade between the metal content of the base and the helium content
above it, but the metal side of the trade has become small
(Sect.~\ref{sec:cnocooling}) and what is left is the helium.
Helium in these solutions is not only the absorber; it is also the
thermostat that sets how far the absorber is carried.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What the wind is made of, and how ionized it is.}
Fig.~\ref{fig:compprof} carries the elemental fractions and the ionization
state of the same three solutions, on the same advection-corrected profiles
as Table~\ref{tab:thermostat}. Two things follow from it that the thermostat
argument rests on.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Diffusion keeps the wind less helium-rich than the
reservoir.} On both branches the helium fraction falls through the
line-forming region and then flattens: ${\rm He/H} = 2.09$ at the base gives
$0.702$ by nuclei at $2\,R_p$ and $0.558$ at $5\,R_p$; $10.01$ gives $3.724$
and $2.856$. That is a factor $3.0$ and $2.7$ between the reservoir and the
wind at $2\,R_p$. The consequence is worth stating in the composition's own
terms: the flux-closed solution at $2.09$ (a reservoir that is $68$
per cent helium by number) has a \emph{hydrogen-dominated} line-forming
region, $n_{\rm H}/n_{\rm tot} = 0.59$ against
$n_{\rm He}/n_{\rm tot} = 0.41$ at $2\,R_p$. At $10.01$ helium leads
there, $0.79$ against $0.21$, but by a factor $2.7$ less than the reservoir
states. A composition quoted for this planet therefore has to say at which
level it is quoted; the reservoir and the absorbing layer are not the same
number, and the memo's crossings are reservoir values.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{The hotter solution is also the more ionized one.} At
$2\,R_p$, $x({\rm H\,\textsc{ii}}) = 0.031$ on the photochemical base at
$2.09$ and $0.057$ at $10.01$: a factor $1.9$, in the run whose neutral
hydrogen density is $3.8$ times lower. Both layers are optically thin at
$13.6$\,eV, so the reading that thinner hydrogen shields itself less does
not account for it; what grows is the ionization of hydrogen \emph{by
helium}, through Penning ionization He($2\,^3S$)$+$H, charge exchange
He$^+ +$H and the He-recombination continuum above $24.6$\,eV. \emph{The
split between those channels cannot be settled from the outputs}: the
individual rates (direct $\Gamma_{\rm H\,\textsc{i}}$, the
He-recombination-driven addition to it, and the secondary-ionization yield)
are not written to any output file. The one channel that can be excluded
outright is $n=2$ photoionization: \texttt{heat\_Hpe[excitedH]} is exactly
zero at every cell of all three runs. Helium ionization itself tracks the
temperature more simply: $x({\rm He\,\textsc{ii}})$ is $0.046$, $0.046$
and $0.055$ at $2\,R_p$ across the three, and
$x({\rm He\,\textsc{iii}})$ is at most $1.2\times10^{-4}$ at $2\,R_p$
($8.9\times10^{-5}$, $8.9\times10^{-5}$ and $1.2\times10^{-4}$).

\smallskip
\noindent Fig.~\ref{fig:beststruct} shows the same kind of structure for the
single well-mixed solution that reproduces the line with no diffusion and no
base metals; Fig.~\ref{fig:compprof} is the set the lower-boundary axis is
built on, so the two are complementary rather than alternative views.

