measured at $2.09$: at $K_{zz} = 10^{9}$ the eddy-mixed column reaches the
microbar match, so the escape flux does not set the composition there. What
the ladder adds is the line.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{There is no crossing.} The lowest rung solved,
$\mathrm{He/H} = 2.0924$, already makes $EW = 1.5006$\,\%\,\AA{} against the
measured $1.108 \pm 0.030$: $35$ per cent, that is $13\sigma$, above it. The
ladder rises from there and flattens near $2.27$\,\%\,\AA{} above
$\mathrm{He/H} \simeq 8$, so the measurement is not reached anywhere on it.
Read against the ladder's own local slope
$\mathrm{d}\ln EW/\mathrm{d}\ln(\mathrm{He/H}) = 0.49$ between the two
lowest rungs, the measured equivalent width would be reached near
$\mathrm{He/H} \approx 1.1$; that is an extrapolation below every solved
rung and is not offered as the answer. \emph{What the ladder does say is
that the photochemical lower boundary no longer makes the line harder to
reach than the metal-free one does: it makes it easier.} The comparison at
one composition is Table~\ref{tab:thermostat}, and its cause is the
density-resolved C, N and O cooling of Sect.~\ref{sec:cnocooling}.

The ladder is shallow and becomes shallower: the local slope runs $0.49$,
$0.32$, $0.15$, $0.08$, $0.05$, $0.03$, $0.04$ and $0.01$ over the
successive intervals, so above $\mathrm{He/H} \simeq 7$ the line stops
responding to composition at all. That saturation is itself a result: on
this base the equivalent width is a weak lever on the reservoir at the
helium-rich end, and a composition read there would carry an enormous
error bar even if the ladder did cross.

At the lowest rung the escape rate is $\log_{10}\dot M = 7.92$, i.e.\
$8.3\times10^{7}$\,g\,s$^{-1}$, a factor $2.4$ below the
$2.03^{+0.58}_{-0.67}\times10^{8}$\,g\,s$^{-1}$ the p-winds retrieval
imposes, and the elemental fluxes are $F_{\rm H} = 2.6\times10^{7}$ and
$F_{\rm He} = 5.7\times10^{7}$\,g\,s$^{-1}$.

\medskip
\noindent Two statements follow, and they are worth separating.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The closure by itself does not select a reservoir on this
      planet.} Every rung of Table~\ref{tab:closureladder} satisfies it, at
      elemental residuals of $2.5$--$4.7\%$ against the $5\%$ tolerance.
      What used to select one was the line, exactly as in
      Sects.~\ref{sec:ew} and~\ref{sec:diffbroad}; on the current code the
      line selects none of them, and the composition this base would need
      lies below the ladder.
\item \textbf{The escape rate is flat along the whole ladder}, $7.91$ to
      $7.92$ in $\log_{10}\dot M$ over a factor $5.7$ in reservoir, so here
      too the wind does not fix the composition, and it agrees with the
      $7.84$--$7.88$ of the prescribed-base branch to within $0.05$\,dex:
      \emph{what is placed at the lower boundary is no longer visible in the
      escape rate either.}
\end{itemize}

The elemental residual $\varepsilon$ of Table~\ref{tab:closureladder} is the
test the closure applies; the radial spread of the flux over the window it
is measured on is $3.7\times10^{-3}$ in hydrogen and $4.9\times10^{-3}$ in
helium.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{What the ladder does not fix, and what it inherits.} The
line is still too narrow: FWHM $0.26$--$0.30$\,\AA{} against the measured
$0.841$, unchanged from Sect.~\ref{sec:diffbroad} and from the
diffusion-off solutions. And the winds these numbers come from carry the
caveats of Sect.~\ref{sec:validity} in full: their domain stops inside the
critical point, and nothing in their profiles above about $10\,R_p$ is
usable. The He\,10830 layer is not affected: it sits at
$\mathrm{Kn} \le 0.011$.

\subsection{The outer region a converged wind inherits}
\label{sec:outerinherit}

\emph{Every number in this subsection is the 2026-08-30 measurement}, read
from \texttt{LHS1140b/archive\_20260830/}. The mechanism it establishes does
not depend on the physics that has changed since
(Sect.~\ref{sec:whatchanged}), but its magnitudes have not been re-measured
on the current code.

The effect was found on the earlier ladder, at its $12.01$ rung, and it is
why the ladder of Table~\ref{tab:closureladder} is climbed from one common
parent. That rung was first reached in one step from the $8.0$ rung, a
$1.50\times$ jump in reservoir; a control run reached the same reservoir
from $11.11$, a $1.08\times$ step. The two solutions agree on
everything the closure and the chemistry set, and disagree on the line:

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Same reservoir}: He/H at the match $12.013522$ against
      $12.013520$, seven digits.
\item \emph{Same lower atmosphere}: cold-trap O/H $1.0656\times10^{-6}$
      against $1.0660\times10^{-6}$ nuclei per H.
\item \emph{Both converged}: \texttt{info}~$=0$ at
      $\|R\| = 1.866\times10^{-4}$ (jump-seeded) and $1.287\times10^{-4}$
      (control), both inside the $4.0\times10^{-4}$ target the rung was run
      to, and both inside the closure's own flux-spread tolerance.
\item \emph{Different line}: $EW = 1.4293$ against $1.2094$\,\%\,\AA{} and a
      red depth of $4.789$ against $3.956$ per cent, an $18$ per cent
      difference in equivalent width at fixed composition.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The difference sits entirely in the outer wind. Two diagnostics
that do not depend on where a spectral window is placed separate the two
solutions, and both are monotonic along the ladder except at the discarded
point:

