\noindent\textbf{LHS\,1140\,b's wind does reach a critical point, and the
domain stops short of it.} All three solutions stay subsonic out to
$29\,R_p$, at $\mathcal{M}_{\max} = 0.34$--$0.37$, so inside the domain the
mass flux is set by the outer boundary condition and not by a critical
point. Extending the domain settles what that costs
(\texttt{docs/lhs1140b\_stationary\_L8\_20260913.md}): at $45$ and
$60\,R_p$ the same case crosses its critical point at $40.06$ and
$40.08\,R_p$ and leaves the domain supersonic, and $\log_{10}\dot M$ is
$7.8737$, $7.8740$ and $7.8735$ at $30$, $45$ and $60\,R_p$ --- a spread of
$5\times10^{-4}$\,dex. The escape rate is therefore not an artifact of where
the boundary was put; the profiles above $10\,R_p$ are ($25$ per cent in
temperature, $29$ in density and $22$ in velocity at $28\,R_p$), and nothing
is quoted from them.

What remains unvalidated is the \emph{continuum} description out there.
$\mathrm{Kn}_{\rm bulk}$ passes $0.1$ at $7.3$--$7.5\,R_p$ and reaches
$0.67$--$0.70$ at the top of the $30\,R_p$ domain, and the exobase of the
extended solutions sits at $29.0\,R_p$, \emph{below} the critical point at
$40.06$. The continuum equations therefore fix their own topology in gas
they cannot describe. The escape rates quoted throughout this memo,
$\log_{10}\dot M \simeq 7.8$--$7.9$, are \textbf{unvalidated in the
collisional sense}, and their insensitivity to the domain size is a
statement about the discrete problem rather than a physical validation.

Against the p-winds retrieval, whose isothermal Parker solution places its
sonic point at $8$--$9.5\,R_p$: $\mathrm{Kn}_{\rm bulk}$ there is
$0.12$--$0.17$, past the threshold. So the finding is neither ``the sonic
point is above the exobase'' nor ``the critical region is collisional'':
the critical region of LHS\,1140\,b is \emph{transitional}. The scale
separation the continuum equations need has been lost by the radius at which
either code sets the mass flux, while the gas is not yet free molecular.

